---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import portrait from '../../assets/about/shiv-kumar.webp';
import SectionLayout from '../../layouts/SectionLayout.astro';
---

<SectionLayout
  title="About"
  description="I work in software engineering and applied AI engineering, with a focus on architecture, document intelligence, and verifiable systems."
  seed="about"
>
  <div slot="hero" class="about-portrait">
    <Image src={portrait} alt="Shiv Kumar" widths={[420, 840]} sizes="336px" loading="eager" />
  </div>
  <section class="prose">
    <p>
      I'm Shiv Kumar. I work as a Senior Software Solutions Architect and a Senior
      Applied AI Engineer &amp; Architect. I'm passionate about how systems are designed,
      how their intent is expressed, and how we establish that they do what we need
      them to do.
    </p>

    <h2>Software engineering</h2>
    <p>
      I care about clear responsibilities, meaningful abstractions, and code whose
      structure makes its purpose understandable. I care just as much about
      verification: defining the expected behavior and writing functional acceptance
      tests that exercise it at the system boundary.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/pwi/">Programming With Intent</a> brings much of that thinking together.
      I've put years into making design and testing guidelines thorough and specific
      enough to apply consistently. That work has new relevance as AI takes on more
      of the implementation and maintenance of code. I want those systems to work
      against explicit engineering standards, with tests that demonstrate whether
      the software meets its requirements. Human judgment defines the intent and
      the standard of correctness.
    </p>

    <h2>Applied AI engineering</h2>
    <p>
      Much of my applied AI work involves document intelligence: extracting structure
      and metadata, identifying requirements, and turning information spread across
      documents into strategic intelligence people can use. I build pipelines that
      combine model interpretation with processing and validation in code. I also
      explore conversational systems that let people question their documents and
      investigate relationships across them.
    </p>
    <h3>Controlled workflows</h3>
    <p>
      One problem I keep returning to is how to get greater determinism and
      repeatability from systems built around language models. My approach is to
      build directed graphs whose controllers and orchestrators are imperative code.
      Code manages dependencies, branching, validation, and failure handling. I use
      models where interpretation, judgment, or generation is required, and keep
      exact operations in code. This is how I approach complex document pipelines
      and software verification workflows.
    </p>
    <p>
      My work with mixtures of experts assigns extraction, independent review,
      diagnosis, and correction to separate specialists. Each has a defined
      responsibility and a constrained view of the evidence. Model judgments can
      still vary; explicit control over execution and acceptance makes the system
      more predictable and its results easier to inspect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Decision models such as <a href="/writing/jev-practical-reference/">Jev</a>
      extend that approach. They make fast, bounded judgments about text and return
      structured decisions and probabilities that code can act on. That gives a
      controller access to semantic judgments that would be difficult to express
      as fixed rules, while keeping control of the workflow in code.
    </p>

    <h3>Agentic systems</h3>
    <p>
      I'm equally interested in systems where agents have room to decide how to
      pursue a goal. Anthea, my personal chief-of-staff project, is an important
      part of that work. Its thesis-defense system adapts to the position someone
      submits. It examines the thesis for gaps, assumptions, and leaps between
      categories of claims, researches the wider subject, and creates specialist
      agents suited to challenge it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each agent has its own persona, perspective, directive, and goal. Within its
      role, it can use tools, seek evidence on the internet, and develop a sustained
      attack on the argument. The opposition takes shape around the thesis being
      examined. Building and working with that system has taught me a great deal
      about my own thinking: the assumptions I leave unstated, how I respond to
      objections, and what it takes to defend a position under serious scrutiny.
    </p>

    <h2>What I share here</h2>
    <p>
      This site brings those interests together through articles, worked examples,
      and material from my videos. I explain the reasoning behind design choices,
      the problems they address, and where they apply. The
      <a href="/writing/">writing</a> covers software engineering and applied AI;
      <a href="/pwi/">Programming With Intent</a> and
      <a href="/acceptance-testing/">Acceptance Testing</a> collect the detailed
      engineering guidance, with examples in Python and C#.
    </p>
  </section>
</SectionLayout>

<style>
  .about-portrait {
    width: clamp(12rem, 40vw, 15rem);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    margin-inline: auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .about-portrait :global(img) {
    /* Frame the face and hand while preserving the complete source photograph. */
    display: block;
    width: 140%;
    max-width: none;
    height: auto;
  }
</style>
